      "end_time": 0,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "With her books such as “Travesties-kamikaze”, “Maîtresses-Cherokees”, and “Danseuses-mamelouk”, poet Josée Yvon (March 31, 1950 – June 12, 1994) was the reigning queen of Montreal’s counter-culture, and cherishes a great ongoing popularity and literary appreciation."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Alan Lord Commentary",
          "tag": "Biographical Context"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "7ae8a52a-0221-497d-9b41-b4a8af2048af"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 65,
      "end_time": 65,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Josée was fond of self-medication, and was visibly and audibly stoned for this performance, teetering and slurring her words."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Alan Lord Commentary",
          "tag": "Personal Anecdote"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "e8abab13-ebcd-456e-88ae-a4391e9a62c4"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 114,
      "end_time": 114,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "When I met Josée, she was stoned on Mandrax, and caressing a pet tarantula on her lap."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Alan Lord Commentary",
          "tag": "Personal Anecdote"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "93d1a1cd-e425-4859-96eb-cacd6f0503f1"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 185,
      "end_time": 185,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Josée Yvon died of AIDS at the age of 44, becoming blind at the end."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
